    "audit_note": "{\"reason\": \"ISIS 353512 ASO caused IL-6 and CRP increases and PBMC immune activation linked to TLR signaling; abstract-only named ASO so Grade B.\", \"grounding_quote\": \"A human peripheral blood mononuclear cell (PBMC)-based assay was developed to identify antisense oligonucleotide (ASO) with the potential to activate a cellular innate immune response outside of an acceptable level. The development of this assay was initiated when ISIS 353512 targeting the messenger ribonucleic acid for human C-reactive protein (CRP) was tested in a phase I clinical trial, in which healthy human volunteers unexpectedly experienced increases in interleukin-6 (IL-6) and CRP. This level of immune stimulation was not anticipated following rodent and nonhuman primate safety studies in which no evidence of exaggerated proinflammatory effects were observed. The IL-6 increase induced by ISIS 353512 was caused by activation of B cells. The IL-6 induction was inhibited by chloroquine pretreatment of PBMCs and the nature of ASOs suggested that the response is mediated by a Toll-like receptor (TLR), in all likelihood TLR9.\", \"source_pmid\": \"35976085\", \"candidate_domain\": \"toxicity\"}",
    "audited_at": "2026-06-07T23:20:28.232879+00:00"
